# Who Wired This - Input & UI Rules

## Input Source of Truth
- `PlayerInputBridge` is the primary source of gameplay intent state
- `WhoWiredThis.Player.PlayerActions` uses `PlayerInputBridge` for inventory/help/menu intent
- Interaction trigger in `PlayerActions` currently comes from `ThirdPersonMixamo.PlayerController.InteractPressedThisFrame`
- If input ownership changes, update both `PlayerActions` and HUD help text to keep prompts consistent
- Legacy `UnityEngine.Input` checks are still intentional in some systems (`MessagePanel`, scene/layout toggles, ThirdPerson movement)

## Pointer/UI Guardrails
- `PlayerInputBridge` exposes `InteractPressedFromPointerThisFrame` for pointer-specific interaction gating
- If click-through-on-UI becomes an issue, handle it in `PlayerActions` (single interaction authority) instead of scattering UI checks across interactables

## HUD and Panels
- `HUDController` owns top-bar and panel toggles (`ToggleInventory`, `ToggleHelp`, `ToggleMenuPanel`)
- `MessagePanel` owns popup/confirmation behavior
- `HUDController.SetInteractKeyLabel(...)` feeds help/prompt display for the active interact key
- New UI behavior should integrate through these existing controllers before adding new panel managers

## UI Binding Conventions
- Use null-safe listener setup for optional inspector refs (`button?.onClick.AddListener(...)`)
- Keep display refresh logic isolated in dedicated methods (`RefreshScore`, `RefreshTimer`, etc.)
- Prefer one-way manager-to-UI updates via events, not polling
- For panels with keyboard confirm/cancel flow, keep the key handling centralized in `MessagePanel`

## Restart/Scene Behavior
- Restart currently reloads active scene name through `SceneManager.LoadScene(activeScene.name)`
- Scene hotkey switching (`SceneHotkeySwitcher`) also uses single-scene loads; new UI reset behavior must remain compatible with full scene reload semantics
